### Introduction to HLD 
System design is divided into two parts :- 
1. HLD (High Level Design) 
2. LLD (Low Level Design)

#### 1. HLD (High Level Design) :- 
HLD focuses on designing arichitecture  of any application. Lets understand the general meaning of an architecture so taking the example of home building what we need to develop this house like Cement, Bricks, Floor internal architecture(rooms,kitchen). Rather than developing the house directly we understand the reqirements or ingridiant we need to develop the house. 
- Building an application where application may be any thing like website, mobile app, etc Before developing the any application we ask some question related to the application like :  
1. Teck stack we need? (MERN, Spring Boot, Jhango,etc on the basis of different languages) , -> So we need to identify and give the reason to use your stack to build your application. 
 - The stack must give some some benefits :- 
   - If the application is based on multi-threaded environment -> Means multiple users  using the critical resource at same time like On BookMyShow multiple users try to block the same seat. -> For this type of application we avoid the python based application as it is not good multi-thread environment. 
2. Cost Optimization :- Cost of deployment of your application per month, Is we able to make money we spend on the deployment of the application? 
3. Database we use in the application? (sql, nosql, graphSQL, etc) 
4. Handling the users of application from thousands to millions. So whenever the sells on the amazon, flipkart we be launch then multiple users comes to the application, etc
 - Estimation of the uses on application will be done called Back of the envelope esitmate. In this we understand that on backend we are creating application How many users we are able to handle. 
 - Real world Ex :-  If 10 floor ka bulilding bana rahe hai then ham ye estimate karenge ki base kitna majbbot hona chahiye. So if pata hai ki ham 2 floor ka ghar hai then us type ka base rakhenge. 
 - If having the estimate of 2 lakhs users and user now coming is 10 lakhs then in this case we may have to the migration of the database, stack, etc like 2 floor ke ghar ko agar 5 manjila banana ho toh ,  base majbbot nahi hai then pure floor ko thode phir se banao naya or we migrate some other place and create 5 floor. 
 - Servers ko scale karna is easy task, but scaling in the stack and databases are complex task. 

#### LLD(Low level Design) :- 
Focus the actual coding part. In this we take care of the How OOPs priciple is being folloewed, Which design principle we use , which design pattern we use, etc
- Code in the application should be :- 
  - Clear Code
  - Loosely coupled code -> simple to add new feature, etc

#### HLD --> Architectire (application) :- 
- Whenever we talk about any softaware/ application -> which may be website, Mobile Application , windows based application,etc so it is generic term. So whenever we talk about the High Level Desion we mainly focus on the website and mobile application because software like photoshop which exist in our computer in offline mode, if photoshop wants to add new feature as a bug fix karne ke bahne software download karne ko bolega. 
-In this cases users are having the choice to integrate the new feature or not. So in software as a developer we cannot integrate quickly. So when use software which take the our harddisk space and ram and more access on our computer -> here we are not have the server and we are not using internet calls so we cannot talk much about the harddisk in this case. 
- So designing the software we are most focused on the LLD part rather than focusing on HLD. 
- So when we have calls using the internet like http calls, etc then we focused on the HLD. Because here we have the server and need to scale this server. 

#### Client-server / request-response  model
- When we are having the application in Internet like Website. On the basic level we have two things :- 
1. Client 
2. Server 

- Client :- When we try to start or try to access any website then  we go to web browser as a client when we type www.xyz.com or hit the URL what happend to the backend. 
 - Ip address fetching
 - our request hit to the server (server is like a computer(powerful)) 

- We know that to run any application we need Ram, CPU, harddisk space, etc are required. In last example of the photoshop we are using our own harddisk, cpu , ram to run that and when we run a website like youtube we are only able to show the video only able to brawse the video, where it is loaded, where it is processe,etc Where this all happen, that will be called the servers(it serves our request).

- When client request(http/https) it get processed on the server , after the processed the output/response is given back to the client. -> called the request-response or client-server model etc. 

- client may be :- browser / mobile application.
- so when we want run the youtube using the mobile app then that mobile app will also work as an client, basically client may be the any frontend through which we are able to do the any http request on server , where it processed and we have getting the response back called request/ respose cycle. 

- Lets say we have the 1000 of users, then they come to the browser then they do the http request they go to the our server(ek he server hai). 
 - We know that server is a computer which process our request at the backend. These server may have their limitations like say it have the RAM: 32 GB , and we are having the 1000 of user comes to our server and this RAM is used up then How we are going to manage the upcoming any user? 
 - Then system will be crashed in that case. To minimize this or to dissolve this case, HLD is based on the procedure called the distributed sysytem. 
 - So computer ke accessories kabhi n kabhi to maximize to ho jayegi, kyuki hamare users bad rahe hai to ham kab tak apne computer ko upgrade karte jayenge. 
 - Hamko kabhi n akbhi toh servers ko aapas mai jodlar ek sath use karna hoga , so is purre process ko jisme ham apne servers ko apas mai jodkar use karte hai n us sysytem ko ham distributed system kahte hai. 
 
 #### Horizontal Scaling vs Vertical Scaling :- 
 - So in the privious example when we reached the maximum RAM  we increing the RAM in 32, 64, 128, 264, etc order mai. (Vertical scaling) 
 - We can also add the servers like server1, server2 ,etc apas mai milkar clients ke request ko handle karnege. (Horizontal)
  - Jisme apne servers ke RAM , harddisk(space) ko badaye ja rahe hai, CPU ko , OS ko better karte ja rahe hai is tarah ke scaling ko vertical scaling kahte hai. We can Imagine that ek he server ko ram, etc accssories ko badaye ja rahe hai. 
  - vertical scaling is good where we have the fixed or less no user interaction is done. 
  - If want to our applications to be used by the millions of users then we do not do the vertical scaling. 
  Hoeizontal scaling :-  Hamko jab bhi uses limit ke bhar ja raha hai(having maximum users) ham ek naya server appoint kar lete hai. 
  - So ye server apas mai jude kaise honge, jab koi client request marega toh wo server1/server2/server3 par ja sakta hai(on each request) , on the request any server can accept that request , so data toh different servers mai processed hoga then ye request synch kaise hoga? aur server apas mai data interaction kaise kar pa raha hai? 

 - So the horizontal and vertical scaling concept is base for understanding the distributed system. 

#### Distributed System :- 
- So these different servers which we are scaling is called the nodes in distributed sysytem. And we represent in the form of circle. 
- The main features of the distributed sysytem is that we are able to scale for the millions of users like if we reached the maximum no of users then we introduce the new server in the system. 
 - Limitations of distributed system is that it complicate the structure of our website. So how the different nodes in the DS will be able to communicate with each other, To communicate with another nodes they must do calls like http calls. 
 - Why node need to communicate with each other in the DS? For maintaing the data intigrity. 
 - So basically for now we need to understand for now is that Due to the DS our website structure becomes complicated. Whereas it is solving a big problem that for now we can able to manage application for the millions of users. 
 -DS brings problems like how node  interact internally, how they are scale-up and scale-down autonatically, and how they are sharing their data, etc

#### NetWork Communication :- 
- When we doing the reqest to the server and  return the response, then the client and server are interacting with each other. 
 - We heave discussed the photoshop example So We know that photoshop will be running locally in our computer, we know through LLD that when this will be running different classes will be exist there. 
  - This classes need to interact, classes will be interact as in one class another class method would be called. Means they are communication in OPPs. And calling method is very simple and We know how to call method. 
- So here when we are talking about the nodes or the client-server architecture, client is not calling any method of server,  client is calling over the internet to server, server is sending the response over the internet. So client and server are communicating with each-other? There must be some rules and regulations. 
- So there must be some language through which they are able to talk to each other, So the rule and regulation for this is known as the Network Communication. 

N/w Communication are the rules that both the client and server follow to communicate. In n/w communication we have :- 
1. N/W protocols : So here will be learning the protocols and their usecase, and for which application we need which protocols, and how we shift the protocols,etc

 - Any application we are having is based on the iso_OSI model which have the 7 layers :- 
   - Application -> Presentation -> Session -> Transport -> Network -> Data-Link -> Physical Layer
   - This layers are responsible for how the client and server are communicating over the network (Internet)
   - So each layer of this model have their own protocol but here we are discussing about the Application layer and Transport layer which are playing he rolw in the HLD. 

   - Applicattion layer protocol :- HTTP, HTTPs, FTP, SMTP, WebRTC, SFTP, Web-Sockets 
   - Transport layer Protocols :- TCP and UDP

   #### Applicattion layer protocol :- 
   - Definition:- When our application is actually calling through the Internt to server. Means When client calls through the internet then this call will be following which rules? They are http/htttps rules. 
   - HTTP (Hyper-Text Transfer Protocol):- If we want to go in detail of this protocol we can browse the it is connection oriented or not , how it sending the packets,etc
    - So important to understand in HLD is that whenever a client is communicating with server it sends the http call to the server. 
    - Communicating like open new web page, etc
   - HTTPs (Hyper-Text Transfer Protocol secure) : So it is the secured version of the HTTP, So whenever we clicking any line where the authentication of the user, try to user the critical resource then in this cases we are calling the HTTPs. 

   - FTP (File Transfer Protocol):- When we want to transfer a file(document) using internet then we use FTP. So when client want to transfer the document to the server (viceversa) then on that request we would be using this protocol. 
   - SFTP (Secure File Transfer Protocol) :- It is secure, and if you want to go in details have a two ways to communicate, etc
  
   - SMTP(Simple Mail Transfer Protocol):- When we want to tansfer any mail over the internet we use the SMTP and when we want to recive the mail over the internet then POP3 / IMAP is used. 

   - WebRTC :- 
     - In the client-server Architecture, we used to send request and receive the response over the internet using the HTTP. So till now we were doing the One-Way communication like client sends req and server back's res. So what if client can also sends the response to server. for this type of application we use the WebRTC. 
     - Like ChatApplication whatsApp,Telegram, etc
       - So lets we have to client, so if client1 wants to sends the m1 message to client2 then technically what happend is client1 send the message  to server using http protocol and so we got the message m1 at the server So now server wants to communicate to client to sends the message, So server cannot sends the message to client using HTTP.
      - Beacuse HTTP protocol says that only client can able to intiate the communication and server will never able to initate the communication. So while transfering the message from server to client we cannot use the http protocol. For in this case we use the WebRTC Protocol. 

   - WebSockets :- 
     - So till now we were talking about the client-server Architecture. So we have the peer-to-peer interaction/communication will be also done. Peer to Peer Communication means communication done b/w two client without the involvement of the server. 
     - So two clinets wants to communicate with each other directly then in this case we use the WebSocket. 

   #### Transport layer Protocol :- 
   - TCP(Transmission Control Protocol) :- So when you want to communicate with client to server and you want a reliable communication -> means when you send the message from the client to server and you want that message should be transferred/ delivered with 100%. 
    - In that case we will be using the TCP Protocol, -> It breaksown the m1 message into collection of packets like p1, p2, p3 So client sends the packets in one by one (in order) server send's the acknowledgement of the packets. 
    - If not ack recived at clients side it means that packets have not been reached to at server side. 
    - Due to this this ways of communication is slow.  
   
   - UDP(User DataGram Protocol) :- 
     - It is not reliable so it is fast, so here the packets are send by the client and one by one without receiving the any ack at client side. 
     - Video straming(live) type of case is done using the UDP protocol, lag issues -> saare packets nahi pahuche at server side but jo be packets hai uska hamne sense bana liya. 

  #### Conclusion :- 
  - Client :- Web-Browser , Mobile Application
  - Horizontal and Vertical Scaling :- In the horizontal scaling we may have the different numbers of server but in the vertical server we have Only One server and we upgrade the server on requirement based But at some time we cannot increse the efficiency-> we have to stop. 
  - Calling :- 
    - Http/Https  call :- Only client can initiate the http calls when client need to load web-pages.
    - FTP/SFTP call :- When  client want to transfer any file over internet we need this call. 
    - SMTP :- When need to transfer mails 
    - And we are having the different calls realted to different cases. 

  ### DNS (Domain Name System) :- 
  - When clinet wants to communicate with the server it cannot talk directly we have the DNS in between. 
   - Client Firstly talk with the DNS and DNS returns the IP address of the server

  - Why client directly unable to communicate with server? Why need the IP address? 
    - Client : Web Browser
    - Server : Youtube (want to interact)

    - So we want to communicate with youtube so we write on our web-brawser as https://www.youtube.com So this url is in the human understandable format, so How internet will bw knowing that ye url kiska hai. 
    - We know that available devices over the internet have to unically Identified as mobile I am using for study shold have some identification over Internet. So this identfication is as the IP address. 
    - IP : 12.34.244.122 -> let say this the IP of the Youtube so to access Youtube we must have this IP. 
    - So human is not comfortable for learning the IP address of the sites as IP address. So we need the mapping . 
    - ex :-  
         https://www.youtube.com      12.34.244.122
         https://www.instagram.com    12.34.244.124

      - we want  that on typing this  https://www.youtube.com we reached to node (12.34.244.122). 
      - So DNS is responsible for  storing this type of mapping. So DNS ek server hai jo mapping karta hai url ko ip address se.
      - DNS have the mapping table which have only two section URL and IP address

      - On hitting the URL we go to the DNS server and then from there we got the IP address of the particular url. So clinet send the URL to the DNS server and server go the table and fetch the IP address that URL and return to the client. 
      - And then client will be request to the server. 
      - We will be leaning it in detail when we will be Doing the HLD project and there in  DNS architecture we will be learning. 
      
         
